{"entity": "Voltmeter", "type": "artifact", "description": "The voltmeter is a measuring instrument used to quantify electrical potential difference—commonly referred to as voltage—across two points in an electric circuit. It is universally represented in circuit diagrams by a circle enclosing the letter “V”. Crucially, the voltmeter must be connected in parallel with the component whose voltage is being measured; this includes resistors (e.g., a main 200 kΩ resistor or resistor R), inductors, capacitors, and load resistors (R_L). Multiple descriptions confirm its parallel placement across specific elements: in some contexts, it measures the voltage drop U_Rx across resistor R (as shown in Figures 4.1 and 4.2); in others, it measures voltage U across the capacitor or across R_L, with explicit polarity markings indicating that the positive terminal connects to point a and the negative terminal to point b. One description incorrectly states the voltmeter is placed *in series*; this contradicts fundamental electrical measurement principles and is inconsistent with all other descriptions, which uniformly specify parallel connection—thus the series assertion is an error and is disregarded in the synthesis. The voltmeter’s physical positioning in diagrams varies (e.g., upper branch, lower parallel branch, or bottom center), but its functional role remains consistent: accurate, low-current-draw voltage measurement across targeted components without disrupting circuit operation.<SEP>The voltmeter is an instrument used to measure voltage (U, U1, U2, U′) across components, and its correct usage is part of the experiment's learning objectives.<SEP>A voltmeter is an instrument used to measure voltage; it has finite internal resistance, causing measurement error when connected in a circuit.<SEP>The voltmeter is an instrument used to measure voltage, with an internal resistance of 10 kΩ, whose connection method affects measurement accuracy for the 200 Ω resistor.<SEP>The voltmeter is an instrument used to measure voltage; it is disconnected during current measurement to avoid internal resistance effects."}
{"entity": "Ammeter", "type": "artifact", "description": "The ammeter is an electrical measurement instrument specifically designed to measure electric current, denoted as I or Iₓ, flowing through a circuit. It is always connected in series with the component or branch whose current is being measured—such as a main 200 kΩ resistor, a load resistor Rₗ, an equivalent resistance Rₑq, or the entire series RLC circuit—ensuring that the same current passes through both the component and the instrument. In circuit diagrams, the ammeter is conventionally represented by a circular symbol labeled “A”, often positioned near the top center of the schematic to indicate its role in measuring total circuit current. Its function is central to experimental learning objectives involving current measurement and circuit analysis. Importantly, although idealized models assume zero internal resistance, real-world ammeters possess non-zero internal resistance, which introduces measurable error into current readings—a critical practical consideration when interpreting results or designing precise measurement setups.<SEP>The ammeter is an instrument used to measure current, with an internal resistance of 0.2 Ω, whose placement relative to the voltmeter influences total measurement error."}
{"entity": "Resistor", "type": "artifact", "description": "Resistor is a passive two-terminal electrical component that implements electrical resistance as a fundamental circuit element. It is consistently labeled “R” (and occasionally “R/2”) in schematic diagrams, appearing in figures such as 4.1 and 4.2, and is represented graphically either by a zigzag line symbol or, in some contexts, a rectangular block—both denoting identical functional behavior. As a passive device, it does not generate energy but instead dissipates electrical energy as heat when current flows through it, fulfilling essential roles in current regulation, voltage division, biasing, signal conditioning, and establishing voltage drops (denoted URx). Resistors are used across diverse configurations—including series, parallel, and hybrid arrangements—and are central to foundational circuit analysis experiments, such as first-order RC circuits, where a resistor is connected in parallel with a capacitor between output terminals to illustrate transient and steady-state responses.

The resistor’s behavior is frequency-independent under direct current (DC) conditions and exhibits resistive dominance in alternating current (AC) applications, though its impedance and voltage–current relationship can be experimentally measured in AC contexts—for instance, with a specified resistance value of 1 kΩ. Resistance values vary widely across practical implementations; for example, a 100-ohm resistor is commonly inserted in series with other circuit elements to enable indirect current measurement via the associated voltage drop. Moreover, measurement accuracy is sensitive to instrument placement: meter positioning introduces variability in observed voltage and current distributions, reflecting real-world experimental error. Resistors thus serve both theoretical and applied functions—as standardized schematic symbols, quantifiable physical components with defined ohmic values, and critical variables in experimental pedagogy involving signal generators, oscilloscopes, and circuit characterization.<SEP>Resistors with values of 1kΩ each are used in the experimental circuit to form branches and enable voltage and current measurements."}
